  [38;5;153msession.getUserId()[39m reads through to storage on each call rather than caching in a variable. It's a single small JSON
  parse, and caching would go stale across tabs — you chose [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m specifically so a second tab sees the same
  user, and a cached copy would quietly defeat that.

  [1mError handling.[0m Five cases, and the first is the one that actually bites:

  1. [1mStorage throws.[0m [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m is not always writable — Safari private browsing has historically thrown on [38;5;153msetItem[39m,
     browsers with site data disabled throw on access, and quota errors exist. Every storage call is wrapped; on failure
     the session falls back to an in-memory object for the life of the page and logs once. The app keeps working, the
     identity just doesn't survive a reload. The alternative — letting it throw — would break the login handler on a
     browser setting the user chose, which is worse.
  2. [1mCorrupt stored JSON.[0m [38;5;153mgetUser()[39m parses inside a try/catch. On a parse failure it clears the bad key and returns
     [38;5;153mnull[39m, so one bad write can't wedge the app permanently.
  3. [1mResponse has no [38;5;153muserId[39m.[0m Don't write a partial record. Log a warning and leave the session empty — a stored [38;5;153m{userId:
[39m     [38;5;153mundefined}[39m would make [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m return something falsy but present, which is the kind of thing that costs an
     afternoon later.
  4. [1m[38;5;153mlogin()[39m rejects.[0m Once it's async and eventually does a real [38;5;153mfetch[39m, network failure is a rejection. The handler
     wraps the [38;5;153mawait[39m in try/catch, logs, and writes nothing.
  5. [1m[38;5;153msuccess: false[39m.[0m No session write. Only a successful login establishes identity.

  [1mReading contract for future forms:[0m [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m returns [38;5;153mstring | null[39m. Never [38;5;153mundefined[39m, never throws. Callers branch on
  [38;5;153mnull[39m for "nobody is logged in." A single, boring contract is what makes this usable by the forms you haven't written
  yet.
